    </form>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Refactored form:

```tsx
type FormState = { error: string | null; submitted: string | null };

function AddComment({ postId }: { postId: string }) {
  async function addComment(prev: FormState, formData: FormData): Promise<FormState> {
    const text = String(formData.get('comment') ?? '');
    try {
      await saveComment(postId, text);
      return { error: null, submitted: null };
    } catch {
      return { error: 'Could not save the comment', submitted: text };
    }
  }

  const [state, formAction, pending] = useActionState(addComment, {
    error: null,
    submitted: null,
  });

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input name="comment" defaultValue={state.submitted ?? ''} required />
      <button disabled={pending}>{pending ? 'Saving...' : 'Add'}</button>
      {state.error && <p role="alert">{state.error}</p>}
    </form>
  );
}
```

What React now owns: passing `formAction` to `<form action>` runs the submission inside a transition, so there is no `e.preventDefault()`, no `startTransition`, and no manual `saving` flag — `pending` covers the whole submission. The action is a reducer over its own results, receiving `(previousState, formData)`, and React queues dispatches sequentially so each call sees the previous result.

The reset is the part that is easy to get wrong. React resets uncontrolled fields when the action returns without throwing (https://react.dev/reference/react-dom/components/form). A *returned* error is a normal return, so `return { error }` on failure would also clear the input — exactly the opposite of the contract. The documented pattern is to return the submitted text alongside the error and feed it to `defaultValue`; React applies the new default before resetting, so the failed comment survives, while the success branch returns `submitted: null` and the reset clears the field. If the action actually throws, the reset does not happen and the error goes to the nearest Error Boundary.

What you still own: catching the expected `saveComment` failure and turning it into state (the original throws). If the API also throws for programmer errors, narrow the catch or rethrow so those reach the boundary and cancel queued actions (https://react.dev/reference/react/useActionState) — this catch-all deliberately treats every failure as a user-facing save error. You also own server-side authorization and validation — the `required` attribute is UX, not security — and the persistence itself. Optional extras: `useOptimistic` to show the comment while it saves, and `useFormStatus` in a nested submit button instead of passing `pending` down.

